(function(m,c){var L=window.ACADEMY.lessons;for(var id in c){Object.assign(L[id],c[id]);}(window.ACADEMY_LOADED=window.ACADEMY_LOADED||{})[m]=1;})("rust-infra",{"rust-infra/why-rust-ownership-borrowing-and-the-mental-model":{"html":"\u003cp>Rust gives the speed of C with memory safety checked at compile time. The price is a stricter way of thinking about who owns each value. Once that model clicks, the compiler becomes a reviewer.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Where Rust fits in infrastructure work\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Ownership: one owner, moved on assignment\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Borrowing: shared and mutable references\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Lifetimes in plain terms\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Reading compiler errors\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"where-rust-fits\">Where Rust fits\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Rust suits tools that must be fast, small and safe: command line utilities, agents that run on every host, proxies, parsers and controllers. A single static binary with no runtime is easy to ship. Go or Python is often simpler for glue scripts, so choose Rust where its guarantees pay off.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"ownership\">Ownership\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Every value has one owner, and the value is dropped when the owner goes out of scope. Assigning a String to another variable moves it, and the first variable can no longer be used. Simple types such as integers are copied instead. This rule removes use after free and double free bugs without a garbage collector.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"borrowing\">Borrowing\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>You can lend a value with a reference. Any number of shared references \u003ccode>&amp;T\u003c/code> may exist, or exactly one mutable reference \u003ccode>&amp;mut T\u003c/code>, never both at once. This prevents data races and surprising changes while someone else reads the value.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"lifetimes\">Lifetimes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A reference must not outlive the value it points to. Usually the compiler infers this. When a function returns a reference, you may need a lifetime annotation such as \u003ccode>&amp;&#39;a str\u003c/code> to say which input it borrows from. If lifetimes feel hard, clone a value or return an owned String first and optimise later.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"compiler-errors\">Compiler errors\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Rust errors are detailed and often suggest the fix. Read the first error fully, since later errors are often caused by it. The common ones are use of a moved value, a borrow that lives too long and a mutable borrow while a shared one exists. Restructure to shorten borrows.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"own&gt;ref;ref&gt;comp;comp&gt;bin\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">Why Rust: ownership, borrowing and the mental model\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"own\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Owner\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"ref\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Borrow\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-security\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"comp\" data-kind=\"security\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Compiler checks\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-store\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"bin\" data-kind=\"store\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Static binary\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Owner to Borrow; 2, Borrow to Compiler checks; 3, Compiler checks to Static binary.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-service\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Service\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-security\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Security\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-store\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Data store\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"own\">\u003cdt>Owner \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Holds the value.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Moved away.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"ref\">\u003cdt>Borrow \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Shared or mutable.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Conflicting borrows.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"comp\">\u003cdt>Compiler checks \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Ownership rules.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Error to read.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"bin\">\u003cdt>Static binary \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Safe and fast.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Large debug build.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-value-used-after-it-moved\">Incident: The value used after it moved\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A first Rust tool failed to compile after a variable was passed to a function and used again.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"cargo build 2&gt;&amp;1 | head -6\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>cargo build 2&gt;&amp;1 | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">head\u003c/span> -6\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">error[E0382]: borrow of moved value: `config`\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">  --&gt; src/main.rs:9:20\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">   |\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\"> 6 |     let config = load();\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\"> 8 |     run(config);\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">(this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The function took ownership of config, so it could not be used afterwards. Passing a reference instead, \u003ccode>run(&amp;config)\u003c/code>, kept the owner in main. The engineer learned to ask whether a function needs to own, borrow or mutate a value.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Cloning everything to silence the compiler\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Fighting the borrow checker instead of changing the design\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Ignoring the first error message\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Using unsafe to avoid a design problem\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Choosing Rust for a ten line script\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"where-rust-fits","text":"Where Rust fits"},{"id":"ownership","text":"Ownership"},{"id":"borrowing","text":"Borrowing"},{"id":"lifetimes","text":"Lifetimes"},{"id":"compiler-errors","text":"Compiler errors"},{"id":"incident-the-value-used-after-it-moved","text":"Incident: The value used after it moved"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"The Rust Programming Language","u":"https://github.com/rust-lang/book"},{"t":"Rust by Example","u":"https://github.com/rust-lang/rust-by-example"},{"t":"Rust repository","u":"https://github.com/rust-lang/rust"}],"text":"Rust gives the speed of C with memory safety checked at compile time. The price is a stricter way of thinking about who owns each value. Once that model clicks, the compiler becomes a reviewer. What you will learn Where Rust fits in infrastructure work Ownership: one owner, moved on assignment Borrowing: shared and mutable references Lifetimes in plain terms Reading compiler errors Where Rust fits Rust suits tools that must be fast, small and safe: command line utilities, agents that run on every host, proxies, parsers and controllers. A single static binary with no runtime is easy to ship. Go or Python is often simpler for glue scripts, so choose Rust where its guarantees pay off. Ownership Every value has one owner, and the value is dropped when the owner goes out of scope. Assigning a String to another variable moves it, and the first variable can no longer be used. Simple types such as integers are copied instead. This rule removes use after free and double free bugs without a garbage collector. Borrowing You can lend a value with a reference. Any number of shared references &T may exist, or exactly one mutable reference &mut T , never both at once. This prevents data races and surprising changes while someone else reads the value. Lifetimes A reference must not outlive the value it points to. Usually the compiler infers this. When a function returns a reference, you may need a lifetime annotation such as &&#39;a str to say which input it borrows from. If lifetimes feel hard, clone a value or return an owned String first and optimise later. Compiler errors Rust errors are detailed and often suggest the fix. Read the first error fully, since later errors are often caused by it. The common ones are use of a moved value, a borrow that lives too long and a mutable borrow while a shared one exists. Restructure to shorten borrows. Why Rust: ownership, borrowing and the mental model Flow in order: 1, Owner to Borrow; 2, Borrow to Compiler checks; 3, Compiler checks to Static binary. Service Step Security Data store Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Owner Service What it does: Holds the value. If it fails or is weak: Moved away. Borrow Step What it does: Shared or mutable. If it fails or is weak: Conflicting borrows. Compiler checks Security What it does: Ownership rules. If it fails or is weak: Error to read. Static binary Data store What it does: Safe and fast. If it fails or is weak: Large debug build. Incident: The value used after it moved Incident A first Rust tool failed to compile after a variable was passed to a function and used again. $ cargo build 2>&1 | head -6 error[E0382]: borrow of moved value: `config` --> src/main.rs:9:20 | 6 | let config = load(); 8 | run(config); (this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident) The function took ownership of config, so it could not be used afterwards. Passing a reference instead, run(&config) , kept the owner in main. The engineer learned to ask whether a function needs to own, borrow or mutate a value. Common mistakes Cloning everything to silence the compiler Fighting the borrow checker instead of changing the design Ignoring the first error message Using unsafe to avoid a design problem Choosing Rust for a ten line script"},"rust-infra/errors-option-result-and-the-question-mark":{"html":"\u003cp>Rust has no exceptions and no null. Missing values and failures are ordinary values that the compiler makes you handle, which is why tools written in Rust rarely crash on bad input.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Option for values that may be missing\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Result for operations that may fail\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>The question mark operator\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Error types with thiserror and anyhow\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Panics versus errors\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"option\">Option\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>Option&lt;T&gt;\u003c/code> is either \u003ccode>Some(value)\u003c/code> or \u003ccode>None\u003c/code>. A map lookup returns an Option, because the key may be absent. You must say what to do for None, using \u003ccode>match\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>if let\u003c/code>, or helpers such as \u003ccode>unwrap_or\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>map\u003c/code>. There is no null pointer to forget.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"result\">Result\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>Result&lt;T, E&gt;\u003c/code> is either \u003ccode>Ok(value)\u003c/code> or \u003ccode>Err(error)\u003c/code>. Reading a file returns a Result, because it may fail. The error type says why, for example the file was missing or permission was denied. Ignoring a Result gives a compiler warning.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"the-question-mark\">The question mark\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Inside a function that returns a Result, \u003ccode>?\u003c/code> after an expression returns early with the error if there was one, and otherwise gives the Ok value. It replaces long chains of match statements and keeps the happy path readable.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"error-types\">Error types\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>For libraries, define an error enum with the \u003ccode>thiserror\u003c/code> crate, so callers can match on the cause. For applications and command line tools, \u003ccode>anyhow::Result\u003c/code> carries any error and lets you add context with \u003ccode>.context(&quot;reading config&quot;)\u003c/code>. Good context turns a message such as No such file into a useful report.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"panics\">Panics\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A panic stops the thread and is for bugs, such as an impossible state. Do not use \u003ccode>unwrap\u003c/code> on input from users or files. In tools that run unattended, prefer returning errors and a clear exit code.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"in&gt;res;res&gt;q;q&gt;out\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">Errors: Option, Result and the question mark\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-external\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"in\" data-kind=\"external\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Input or file\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Outside party\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"res\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Result\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"q\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Question mark\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"out\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Exit code and message\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Input or file to Result; 2, Result to Question mark; 3, Question mark to Exit code and message.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-external\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Outside party\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-service\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Service\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"in\">\u003cdt>Input or file \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Outside party\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> May be missing.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Bad data.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"res\">\u003cdt>Result \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Ok or Err.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Ignored.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"q\">\u003cdt>Question mark \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Propagates errors.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> No context.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"out\">\u003cdt>Exit code and message \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Clear failure.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Panic.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-a-panic-at-three-in-the-morning\">Incident: A panic at three in the morning\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A cleanup tool crashed on an unexpected file name and left a job half done.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"./cleanup --dir /data/tmp\necho $?\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>./cleanup --\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">dir\u003c/span> /data/tmp\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">thread 'main' panicked at src/main.rs:31:46:\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">called `Result::unwrap()` on an `Err` value: Os { code: 13, kind: PermissionDenied }\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">echo\u003c/span> $?\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">101\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">(this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The code used unwrap on a file operation. It was changed to return a Result with context that names the path, the tool now reports the error and continues with the next file, and the exit code distinguishes partial failure from success.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>unwrap on user input and files\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Errors with no context\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Using panic for expected failures\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Returning strings as errors\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Ignoring Results with let underscore\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"option","text":"Option"},{"id":"result","text":"Result"},{"id":"the-question-mark","text":"The question mark"},{"id":"error-types","text":"Error types"},{"id":"panics","text":"Panics"},{"id":"incident-a-panic-at-three-in-the-morning","text":"Incident: A panic at three in the morning"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"anyhow","u":"https://github.com/dtolnay/anyhow"},{"t":"thiserror","u":"https://github.com/dtolnay/thiserror"},{"t":"The Rust Programming Language","u":"https://github.com/rust-lang/book"}],"text":"Rust has no exceptions and no null. Missing values and failures are ordinary values that the compiler makes you handle, which is why tools written in Rust rarely crash on bad input. What you will learn Option for values that may be missing Result for operations that may fail The question mark operator Error types with thiserror and anyhow Panics versus errors Option Option\u003cT> is either Some(value) or None . A map lookup returns an Option, because the key may be absent. You must say what to do for None, using match , if let , or helpers such as unwrap_or and map . There is no null pointer to forget. Result Result\u003cT, E> is either Ok(value) or Err(error) . Reading a file returns a Result, because it may fail. The error type says why, for example the file was missing or permission was denied. Ignoring a Result gives a compiler warning. The question mark Inside a function that returns a Result, ? after an expression returns early with the error if there was one, and otherwise gives the Ok value. It replaces long chains of match statements and keeps the happy path readable. Error types For libraries, define an error enum with the thiserror crate, so callers can match on the cause. For applications and command line tools, anyhow::Result carries any error and lets you add context with .context(\"reading config\") . Good context turns a message such as No such file into a useful report. Panics A panic stops the thread and is for bugs, such as an impossible state. Do not use unwrap on input from users or files. In tools that run unattended, prefer returning errors and a clear exit code. Errors: Option, Result and the question mark Flow in order: 1, Input or file to Result; 2, Result to Question mark; 3, Question mark to Exit code and message. Outside party Step Service Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Input or file Outside party What it does: May be missing. If it fails or is weak: Bad data. Result Step What it does: Ok or Err. If it fails or is weak: Ignored. Question mark Step What it does: Propagates errors. If it fails or is weak: No context. Exit code and message Service What it does: Clear failure. If it fails or is weak: Panic. Incident: A panic at three in the morning Incident A cleanup tool crashed on an unexpected file name and left a job half done. $ ./cleanup -- dir /data/tmp thread 'main' panicked at src/main.rs:31:46: called `Result::unwrap()` on an `Err` value: Os { code: 13, kind: PermissionDenied } $ echo $? 101 (this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident) The code used unwrap on a file operation. It was changed to return a Result with context that names the path, the tool now reports the error and continues with the next file, and the exit code distinguishes partial failure from success. Common mistakes unwrap on user input and files Errors with no context Using panic for expected failures Returning strings as errors Ignoring Results with let underscore"},"rust-infra/structs-enums-traits-and-generics":{"html":"\u003cp>Enums and traits are where Rust shines for infrastructure code. A state machine written as an enum cannot be in an impossible state, and traits let you swap implementations in tests.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Structs and methods\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Enums that carry data and match\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Traits as shared behaviour\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Generics and trait bounds\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Derive macros for common behaviour\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"structs\">Structs\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A struct groups named fields. Methods are written in an \u003ccode>impl\u003c/code> block, and they take \u003ccode>self\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>&amp;self\u003c/code> or \u003ccode>&amp;mut self\u003c/code> depending on whether they consume, read or change the value. Keep fields private and expose methods when you need to keep invariants.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"enums\">Enums\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Each variant of an enum may carry data. A deployment state such as \u003ccode>Pending\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>Running { pid: u32 }\u003c/code> or \u003ccode>Failed(String)\u003c/code> makes it impossible to read a pid for a failed deployment. A \u003ccode>match\u003c/code> must handle every variant, so adding a variant forces you to update every place that needs it.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"traits\">Traits\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A trait defines methods a type can implement, like an interface. A \u003ccode>Storage\u003c/code> trait with \u003ccode>put\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>get\u003c/code> can have a real implementation for S3 and an in-memory one for tests. Functions can take \u003ccode>&amp;dyn Storage\u003c/code> for runtime choice or a generic parameter for compile time choice.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"generics-and-bounds\">Generics and bounds\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Generic functions work for many types: \u003ccode>fn largest&lt;T: PartialOrd&gt;(items: &amp;[T])\u003c/code>. The bound says what the type must support. Generics are resolved at compile time and cost nothing at run time, but they can make error messages long. Start concrete and generalise when you have two uses.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"derive\">Derive\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>#[derive(Debug, Clone, PartialEq)]\u003c/code> generates common implementations. With serde, \u003ccode>#[derive(Serialize, Deserialize)]\u003c/code> turns a struct into JSON or YAML and back. This replaces a lot of parsing code and checks types at the boundary.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"data&gt;trait;trait&gt;gen;trait&gt;test\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">Structs, enums, traits and generics\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"data\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Structs and enums\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"trait\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Traits\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"gen\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Generics\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-observe\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"test\" data-kind=\"observe\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Test doubles\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Structs and enums to Traits; 2, Traits to Generics; 3, Traits to Test doubles.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-service\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Service\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-observe\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Observability\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"data\">\u003cdt>Structs and enums \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Model state.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Impossible states.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"trait\">\u003cdt>Traits \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Shared behaviour.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Over abstraction.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"gen\">\u003cdt>Generics \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Reusable code.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Long errors.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"test\">\u003cdt>Test doubles \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Swap implementations.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> No trait.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-a-state-that-should-not-exist\">Incident: A state that should not exist\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A deploy tool reported a running service with no process id, because states were booleans and strings.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"grep -n &quot;is_running&quot; src/*.rs | head -3\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>grep -n \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;is_running&quot;\u003c/span> src/*.rs | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">head\u003c/span> -3\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">src/deploy.rs:14:    pub is_running: bool,\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">src/deploy.rs:15:    pub pid: Option&lt;u32&gt;,\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">src/deploy.rs:41:    if d.is_running { println!(&quot;pid {}&quot;, d.pid.unwrap()); }\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">(this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The struct allowed is_running true with pid None, and unwrap panicked. Replacing both fields with an enum whose Running variant carries the pid made that state impossible, and the compiler pointed to every place that needed an update.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Booleans and strings instead of enums\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Traits created before a second implementation exists\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Public fields that break invariants\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Deriving nothing and writing boilerplate\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Generic code that nobody can read\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"structs","text":"Structs"},{"id":"enums","text":"Enums"},{"id":"traits","text":"Traits"},{"id":"generics-and-bounds","text":"Generics and bounds"},{"id":"derive","text":"Derive"},{"id":"incident-a-state-that-should-not-exist","text":"Incident: A state that should not exist"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Rust API guidelines","u":"https://github.com/rust-lang/api-guidelines"},{"t":"The Rust Programming Language","u":"https://github.com/rust-lang/book"},{"t":"Rust by Example","u":"https://github.com/rust-lang/rust-by-example"}],"text":"Enums and traits are where Rust shines for infrastructure code. A state machine written as an enum cannot be in an impossible state, and traits let you swap implementations in tests. What you will learn Structs and methods Enums that carry data and match Traits as shared behaviour Generics and trait bounds Derive macros for common behaviour Structs A struct groups named fields. Methods are written in an impl block, and they take self , &self or &mut self depending on whether they consume, read or change the value. Keep fields private and expose methods when you need to keep invariants. Enums Each variant of an enum may carry data. A deployment state such as Pending , Running { pid: u32 } or Failed(String) makes it impossible to read a pid for a failed deployment. A match must handle every variant, so adding a variant forces you to update every place that needs it. Traits A trait defines methods a type can implement, like an interface. A Storage trait with put and get can have a real implementation for S3 and an in-memory one for tests. Functions can take &dyn Storage for runtime choice or a generic parameter for compile time choice. Generics and bounds Generic functions work for many types: fn largest\u003cT: PartialOrd>(items: &[T]) . The bound says what the type must support. Generics are resolved at compile time and cost nothing at run time, but they can make error messages long. Start concrete and generalise when you have two uses. Derive #[derive(Debug, Clone, PartialEq)] generates common implementations. With serde, #[derive(Serialize, Deserialize)] turns a struct into JSON or YAML and back. This replaces a lot of parsing code and checks types at the boundary. Structs, enums, traits and generics Flow in order: 1, Structs and enums to Traits; 2, Traits to Generics; 3, Traits to Test doubles. Service Step Observability Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Structs and enums Service What it does: Model state. If it fails or is weak: Impossible states. Traits Step What it does: Shared behaviour. If it fails or is weak: Over abstraction. Generics Step What it does: Reusable code. If it fails or is weak: Long errors. Test doubles Observability What it does: Swap implementations. If it fails or is weak: No trait. Incident: A state that should not exist Incident A deploy tool reported a running service with no process id, because states were booleans and strings. $ grep -n \"is_running\" src/*.rs | head -3 src/deploy.rs:14: pub is_running: bool, src/deploy.rs:15: pub pid: Option\u003cu32>, src/deploy.rs:41: if d.is_running { println!(\"pid {}\", d.pid.unwrap()); } (this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident) The struct allowed is_running true with pid None, and unwrap panicked. Replacing both fields with an enum whose Running variant carries the pid made that state impossible, and the compiler pointed to every place that needed an update. Common mistakes Booleans and strings instead of enums Traits created before a second implementation exists Public fields that break invariants Deriving nothing and writing boilerplate Generic code that nobody can read"},"rust-infra/cli-tools-with-clap-serde-and-files":{"html":"\u003cp>A command line tool is the classic first Rust project for operations engineers. Clap and serde provide the hard parts, which leaves you free to focus on the task.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Project layout and Cargo\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Arguments and subcommands with clap\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Reading JSON and YAML with serde\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Output formats and exit codes\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Testing a CLI\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"layout\">Layout\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>cargo new tool\u003c/code> creates a binary crate. Keep \u003ccode>main.rs\u003c/code> small, put logic in a library module and add dependencies with \u003ccode>cargo add clap serde serde_json anyhow\u003c/code>. A library crate with a thin binary is easy to test.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"clap\">Clap\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>With the derive feature, a struct describes the command line and clap generates parsing, help text and error messages. Subcommands are an enum, such as \u003ccode>check\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>apply\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>list\u003c/code>. Add \u003ccode>--output json\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>--dry-run\u003c/code> flags early, because operators expect them.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"serde\">Serde\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Define a struct for the configuration and derive Deserialize. Reading a file with \u003ccode>serde_yaml\u003c/code> or \u003ccode>serde_json\u003c/code> either gives a typed value or an error that names the field. Use \u003ccode>#[serde(deny_unknown_fields)]\u003c/code> to catch typos in config files, and defaults for optional fields.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"output-and-exit-codes\">Output and exit codes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Print results to standard output and diagnostics to standard error, so pipes work. Offer machine readable JSON for scripts. Exit with zero on success and distinct non zero codes for usage errors, partial failure and total failure, and document them.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"testing\">Testing\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Unit test the library functions with ordinary tests. For the binary use the \u003ccode>assert_cmd\u003c/code> crate, which runs it and checks output and exit code. Test the error paths, such as a missing file and invalid configuration, since that is where operators meet your tool.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"args&gt;cfg;cfg&gt;core;core&gt;out\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">CLI tools with clap, serde and files\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-external\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"args\" data-kind=\"external\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">clap arguments\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Outside party\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"cfg\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">serde config\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"core\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Library logic\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"out\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Output and exit code\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, clap arguments to serde config; 2, serde config to Library logic; 3, Library logic to Output and exit code.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-external\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Outside party\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-service\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Service\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"args\">\u003cdt>clap arguments \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Outside party\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Subcommands and flags.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Bad usage.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"cfg\">\u003cdt>serde config \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Typed settings.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Typos.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"core\">\u003cdt>Library logic \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Testable core.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Logic in main.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"out\">\u003cdt>Output and exit code \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> JSON and codes.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Always zero.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-a-tool-that-always-exited-zero\">Incident: A tool that always exited zero\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A CI check built in Rust passed every build because it exited with zero on errors.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"./check-config bad.yaml; echo &quot;exit=$?&quot;\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>./check-config bad.yaml; \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">echo\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;exit=$?&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">error: unknown field `replicas_count`, expected one of `replicas`, `image`\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">exit=0\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">(this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The main function printed the error and returned normally. Changing it to return a Result from main made the process exit with a non zero code. The team added a CLI test that asserts the exit code for invalid input.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Printing errors but exiting zero\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Mixing logs and results on standard output\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>No dry run for changing commands\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Config files with no field validation\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Logic in main that cannot be tested\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"layout","text":"Layout"},{"id":"clap","text":"Clap"},{"id":"serde","text":"Serde"},{"id":"output-and-exit-codes","text":"Output and exit codes"},{"id":"testing","text":"Testing"},{"id":"incident-a-tool-that-always-exited-zero","text":"Incident: A tool that always exited zero"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"clap","u":"https://github.com/clap-rs/clap"},{"t":"serde","u":"https://github.com/serde-rs/serde"},{"t":"Cargo","u":"https://github.com/rust-lang/cargo"}],"text":"A command line tool is the classic first Rust project for operations engineers. Clap and serde provide the hard parts, which leaves you free to focus on the task. What you will learn Project layout and Cargo Arguments and subcommands with clap Reading JSON and YAML with serde Output formats and exit codes Testing a CLI Layout cargo new tool creates a binary crate. Keep main.rs small, put logic in a library module and add dependencies with cargo add clap serde serde_json anyhow . A library crate with a thin binary is easy to test. Clap With the derive feature, a struct describes the command line and clap generates parsing, help text and error messages. Subcommands are an enum, such as check , apply and list . Add --output json and --dry-run flags early, because operators expect them. Serde Define a struct for the configuration and derive Deserialize. Reading a file with serde_yaml or serde_json either gives a typed value or an error that names the field. Use #[serde(deny_unknown_fields)] to catch typos in config files, and defaults for optional fields. Output and exit codes Print results to standard output and diagnostics to standard error, so pipes work. Offer machine readable JSON for scripts. Exit with zero on success and distinct non zero codes for usage errors, partial failure and total failure, and document them. Testing Unit test the library functions with ordinary tests. For the binary use the assert_cmd crate, which runs it and checks output and exit code. Test the error paths, such as a missing file and invalid configuration, since that is where operators meet your tool. CLI tools with clap, serde and files Flow in order: 1, clap arguments to serde config; 2, serde config to Library logic; 3, Library logic to Output and exit code. Outside party Step Service Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. clap arguments Outside party What it does: Subcommands and flags. If it fails or is weak: Bad usage. serde config Step What it does: Typed settings. If it fails or is weak: Typos. Library logic Service What it does: Testable core. If it fails or is weak: Logic in main. Output and exit code Service What it does: JSON and codes. If it fails or is weak: Always zero. Incident: A tool that always exited zero Incident A CI check built in Rust passed every build because it exited with zero on errors. $ ./check-config bad.yaml; echo \"exit=$?\" error: unknown field `replicas_count`, expected one of `replicas`, `image` exit=0 (this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident) The main function printed the error and returned normally. Changing it to return a Result from main made the process exit with a non zero code. The team added a CLI test that asserts the exit code for invalid input. Common mistakes Printing errors but exiting zero Mixing logs and results on standard output No dry run for changing commands Config files with no field validation Logic in main that cannot be tested"},"rust-infra/async-rust-with-tokio-and-network-services":{"html":"\u003cp>Network tools such as proxies, exporters and agents spend most of their time waiting. Async Rust handles thousands of connections on a few threads, if you follow a few rules.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Futures, async and await\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>The Tokio runtime and tasks\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Timeouts, cancellation and select\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Channels and shared state\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Blocking calls and a small HTTP service\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"futures\">Futures\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>An \u003ccode>async fn\u003c/code> returns a future, a value that does nothing until it is awaited. \u003ccode>await\u003c/code> pauses the function until the result is ready and lets other tasks run. Futures are lazy, so forgetting to await a call means it never runs, and the compiler warns about it.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"tokio\">Tokio\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Tokio provides a runtime that schedules tasks over a pool of threads. \u003ccode>#[tokio::main]\u003c/code> starts it, and \u003ccode>tokio::spawn\u003c/code> runs a future as a separate task. Tasks are cheap, so spawn one per connection. Use Tokio versions of file, network and timer operations.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"timeouts-and-cancellation\">Timeouts and cancellation\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Wrap remote calls in \u003ccode>tokio::time::timeout\u003c/code>, because a network call without one can wait forever. Dropping a future cancels it, so be careful about work that must not stop halfway. \u003ccode>tokio::select!\u003c/code> waits for the first of several futures, for example a request or a shutdown signal.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"channels-and-state\">Channels and state\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Tasks communicate through channels such as \u003ccode>mpsc\u003c/code>, which avoids sharing memory. When you do share state, wrap it in \u003ccode>Arc&lt;Mutex&lt;T&gt;&gt;\u003c/code> and keep the lock short and never hold it across an await. For counters prefer atomics.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"blocking-and-http\">Blocking and HTTP\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A blocking call such as a heavy computation or a synchronous file read stops a worker thread and starves other tasks. Run it with \u003ccode>spawn_blocking\u003c/code>. For HTTP, \u003ccode>axum\u003c/code> builds services from handler functions, and \u003ccode>reqwest\u003c/code> is a client. Add graceful shutdown so in-flight requests finish before exit.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"req&gt;rt;rt&gt;task;task&gt;shut\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">Async Rust with Tokio and network services\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-external\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"req\" data-kind=\"external\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Incoming requests\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Outside party\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"rt\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Tokio runtime\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"task\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Tasks and channels\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"shut\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Graceful shutdown\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Incoming requests to Tokio runtime; 2, Tokio runtime to Tasks and channels; 3, Tasks and channels to Graceful shutdown.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-external\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Outside party\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-service\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Service\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"req\">\u003cdt>Incoming requests \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Outside party\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Many connections.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Overload.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"rt\">\u003cdt>Tokio runtime \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Schedules tasks.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Blocked threads.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"task\">\u003cdt>Tasks and channels \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Concurrent work.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Lock held across await.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"shut\">\u003cdt>Graceful shutdown \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Finishes requests.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Abrupt exit.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-a-service-that-froze-under-load\">Incident: A service that froze under load\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>An exporter stopped answering health checks when one handler read a large file.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"curl -s -m 2 localhost:9100/health; echo &quot;exit=$?&quot;\ngrep -n &quot;std::fs::read&quot; src/*.rs\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>curl -s -m 2 localhost:9100/health; \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">echo\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;exit=$?&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">exit=28\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>grep -n \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;std::fs::read&quot;\u003c/span> src/*.rs\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">src/handlers.rs:22:    let data = std::fs::read(path).unwrap();\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">(this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The handler used a blocking file read on the runtime thread, so with a few slow reads all workers were busy and health checks timed out. Using tokio::fs and spawn_blocking for the heavy parse restored responsiveness.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Blocking calls on the runtime\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>No timeout on network calls\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Holding a mutex across an await\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Spawning unbounded tasks without limits\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>No graceful shutdown\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"futures","text":"Futures"},{"id":"tokio","text":"Tokio"},{"id":"timeouts-and-cancellation","text":"Timeouts and cancellation"},{"id":"channels-and-state","text":"Channels and state"},{"id":"blocking-and-http","text":"Blocking and HTTP"},{"id":"incident-a-service-that-froze-under-load","text":"Incident: A service that froze under load"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"tokio","u":"https://github.com/tokio-rs/tokio"},{"t":"The Rust Programming Language","u":"https://github.com/rust-lang/book"},{"t":"Rust by Example","u":"https://github.com/rust-lang/rust-by-example"}],"text":"Network tools such as proxies, exporters and agents spend most of their time waiting. Async Rust handles thousands of connections on a few threads, if you follow a few rules. What you will learn Futures, async and await The Tokio runtime and tasks Timeouts, cancellation and select Channels and shared state Blocking calls and a small HTTP service Futures An async fn returns a future, a value that does nothing until it is awaited. await pauses the function until the result is ready and lets other tasks run. Futures are lazy, so forgetting to await a call means it never runs, and the compiler warns about it. Tokio Tokio provides a runtime that schedules tasks over a pool of threads. #[tokio::main] starts it, and tokio::spawn runs a future as a separate task. Tasks are cheap, so spawn one per connection. Use Tokio versions of file, network and timer operations. Timeouts and cancellation Wrap remote calls in tokio::time::timeout , because a network call without one can wait forever. Dropping a future cancels it, so be careful about work that must not stop halfway. tokio::select! waits for the first of several futures, for example a request or a shutdown signal. Channels and state Tasks communicate through channels such as mpsc , which avoids sharing memory. When you do share state, wrap it in Arc\u003cMutex\u003cT>> and keep the lock short and never hold it across an await. For counters prefer atomics. Blocking and HTTP A blocking call such as a heavy computation or a synchronous file read stops a worker thread and starves other tasks. Run it with spawn_blocking . For HTTP, axum builds services from handler functions, and reqwest is a client. Add graceful shutdown so in-flight requests finish before exit. Async Rust with Tokio and network services Flow in order: 1, Incoming requests to Tokio runtime; 2, Tokio runtime to Tasks and channels; 3, Tasks and channels to Graceful shutdown. Outside party Step Service Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Incoming requests Outside party What it does: Many connections. If it fails or is weak: Overload. Tokio runtime Step What it does: Schedules tasks. If it fails or is weak: Blocked threads. Tasks and channels Service What it does: Concurrent work. If it fails or is weak: Lock held across await. Graceful shutdown Service What it does: Finishes requests. If it fails or is weak: Abrupt exit. Incident: A service that froze under load Incident An exporter stopped answering health checks when one handler read a large file. $ curl -s -m 2 localhost:9100/health; echo \"exit=$?\" exit=28 $ grep -n \"std::fs::read\" src/*.rs src/handlers.rs:22: let data = std::fs::read(path).unwrap(); (this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident) The handler used a blocking file read on the runtime thread, so with a few slow reads all workers were busy and health checks timed out. Using tokio::fs and spawn_blocking for the heavy parse restored responsiveness. Common mistakes Blocking calls on the runtime No timeout on network calls Holding a mutex across an await Spawning unbounded tasks without limits No graceful shutdown"},"rust-infra/testing-cross-compilation-and-shipping-static-binaries":{"html":"\u003cp>A big advantage of Rust is the artifact: one small binary with no runtime. Testing and release engineering make sure that artifact is trustworthy and easy to deploy everywhere.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Unit, integration and doc tests\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Clippy, fmt and cargo audit in CI\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Cross compiling and static linking with musl\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Release profiles and binary size\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Containers, signing and provenance\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"tests\">Tests\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Unit tests live next to the code in a \u003ccode>#[cfg(test)]\u003c/code> module and integration tests in the \u003ccode>tests\u003c/code> directory. Doc tests run the examples in comments. \u003ccode>cargo test\u003c/code> runs all of them, and property testing with \u003ccode>proptest\u003c/code> finds edge cases you did not think of.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"ci-checks\">CI checks\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Run \u003ccode>cargo fmt --check\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>cargo clippy -- -D warnings\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>cargo test\u003c/code> on every change. \u003ccode>cargo audit\u003c/code> checks dependencies against the advisory database, and \u003ccode>cargo deny\u003c/code> can enforce licence and source rules. Cache the build directory to keep CI fast.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"cross-compiling\">Cross compiling\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Add a target with \u003ccode>rustup target add x86_64-unknown-linux-musl\u003c/code> and build with \u003ccode>--target\u003c/code>. The musl target links statically, so the binary runs on any Linux without a matching libc. Tools such as \u003ccode>cross\u003c/code> or \u003ccode>cargo zigbuild\u003c/code> handle linkers and C dependencies for other platforms.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"release-profile\">Release profile\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Set \u003ccode>opt-level\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>lto = true\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>codegen-units = 1\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>strip = true\u003c/code> in the release profile to shrink and speed up the binary at the cost of build time. Measure the size, since a few megabytes is normal and tens of megabytes means unneeded dependencies.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"containers-and-signing\">Containers and signing\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Copy the static binary into a scratch or distroless image for a very small container with little to attack. Generate an SBOM, sign the artifacts and publish checksums. Pin the Rust toolchain version in \u003ccode>rust-toolchain.toml\u003c/code> so builds are repeatable.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"test&gt;build;build&gt;musl;musl&gt;img\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">Testing, cross compilation and shipping static binaries\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"test\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">cargo test and clippy\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"build\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Release build\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"musl\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Static musl target\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-security\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"img\" data-kind=\"security\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Scratch image and signature\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, cargo test and clippy to Release build; 2, Release build to Static musl target; 3, Static musl target to Scratch image and signature.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-service\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Service\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-security\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Security\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"test\">\u003cdt>cargo test and clippy \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Quality gate.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Skipped.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"build\">\u003cdt>Release build \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> LTO, strip.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Slow build.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"musl\">\u003cdt>Static musl target \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> No libc needed.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> C dependencies.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"img\">\u003cdt>Scratch image and signature \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Small and verifiable.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Unsigned.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-a-binary-that-would-not-start-on-the-server\">Incident: A binary that would not start on the server\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A tool built on a developer laptop failed on an older server with a missing library version.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"./agent --version\nldd ./agent | head -3\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>./agent --version\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">./agent: /lib64/libc.so.6: version `GLIBC_2.34' not found (required by ./agent)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>ldd ./agent | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">head\u003c/span> -3\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">    linux-vdso.so.1\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">    libc.so.6 =&gt; /lib64/libc.so.6\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">(this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The binary was linked dynamically against a newer libc than the server had. Building for the musl target in CI produced a static binary that ran everywhere, and the pipeline now tests the artifact in a container with an old distribution.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Building releases on laptops\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Dynamic linking to a newer libc\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>No dependency audit\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Unpinned toolchain\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Huge binaries from unused features\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"tests","text":"Tests"},{"id":"ci-checks","text":"CI checks"},{"id":"cross-compiling","text":"Cross compiling"},{"id":"release-profile","text":"Release profile"},{"id":"containers-and-signing","text":"Containers and signing"},{"id":"incident-a-binary-that-would-not-start-on-the-server","text":"Incident: A binary that would not start on the server"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"cross","u":"https://github.com/cross-rs/cross"},{"t":"cargo-zigbuild","u":"https://github.com/rust-cross/cargo-zigbuild"},{"t":"Cargo","u":"https://github.com/rust-lang/cargo"}],"text":"A big advantage of Rust is the artifact: one small binary with no runtime. Testing and release engineering make sure that artifact is trustworthy and easy to deploy everywhere. What you will learn Unit, integration and doc tests Clippy, fmt and cargo audit in CI Cross compiling and static linking with musl Release profiles and binary size Containers, signing and provenance Tests Unit tests live next to the code in a #[cfg(test)] module and integration tests in the tests directory. Doc tests run the examples in comments. cargo test runs all of them, and property testing with proptest finds edge cases you did not think of. CI checks Run cargo fmt --check , cargo clippy -- -D warnings and cargo test on every change. cargo audit checks dependencies against the advisory database, and cargo deny can enforce licence and source rules. Cache the build directory to keep CI fast. Cross compiling Add a target with rustup target add x86_64-unknown-linux-musl and build with --target . The musl target links statically, so the binary runs on any Linux without a matching libc. Tools such as cross or cargo zigbuild handle linkers and C dependencies for other platforms. Release profile Set opt-level , lto = true , codegen-units = 1 and strip = true in the release profile to shrink and speed up the binary at the cost of build time. Measure the size, since a few megabytes is normal and tens of megabytes means unneeded dependencies. Containers and signing Copy the static binary into a scratch or distroless image for a very small container with little to attack. Generate an SBOM, sign the artifacts and publish checksums. Pin the Rust toolchain version in rust-toolchain.toml so builds are repeatable. Testing, cross compilation and shipping static binaries Flow in order: 1, cargo test and clippy to Release build; 2, Release build to Static musl target; 3, Static musl target to Scratch image and signature. Step Service Security Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. cargo test and clippy Step What it does: Quality gate. If it fails or is weak: Skipped. Release build Step What it does: LTO, strip. If it fails or is weak: Slow build. Static musl target Service What it does: No libc needed. If it fails or is weak: C dependencies. Scratch image and signature Security What it does: Small and verifiable. If it fails or is weak: Unsigned. Incident: A binary that would not start on the server Incident A tool built on a developer laptop failed on an older server with a missing library version. $ ./agent --version ./agent: /lib64/libc.so.6: version `GLIBC_2.34' not found (required by ./agent) $ ldd ./agent | head -3 linux-vdso.so.1 libc.so.6 => /lib64/libc.so.6 (this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident) The binary was linked dynamically against a newer libc than the server had. Building for the musl target in CI produced a static binary that ran everywhere, and the pipeline now tests the artifact in a container with an old distribution. Common mistakes Building releases on laptops Dynamic linking to a newer libc No dependency audit Unpinned toolchain Huge binaries from unused features"}});